+# Auth API broken end-to-end: CORS preflight rejects the app's own headers; register→login dead; server validation gaps
+
+- **Agent**: human-eye (Job 478633, cycle-22, 2026-10-29 03:57+08)
+- **Bundle under test**: `Build 2026-10-27T18:10:28.851Z` / `index-CAV1C5sl.js` (unchanged; findings are server-side)
+- **Companion reports**: human-eye-auth-ux-20261029.md (#2207 — UI-level), human-eye-health-staleness-20261029.md (#2205)
+- **Severity**: **P1 — self-serve activation funnel is 100% dead** (passkey 405 [filed #2207], Google disabled,
+  register browser-blocked [F-12], register-then-login 401 [F-11], zero error feedback [filed #2207 F-7])
+
+## F-12 — CORS preflight rejects the frontend's own custom headers → register & config fail in every real browser (P1)
+
+The Studio bundle (`index-CAV1C5sl.js`) sends custom headers on mutating/config calls:
+`X-CSRF-Token, X-Conversation-Id, X-Correlation-ID, X-Device-Fingerprint, X-JIT-OTP, X-Request-ID, X-SupremeAI-Key, X-Trusted-Browser`.
+
+The API gateway preflight allow-list (`Access-Control-Allow-Headers`) covers only:
+`Accept, Accept-Language, Authorization, Content-Language, Content-Type, Origin, Referer, X-Correlation-ID, X-Forwarded-For, X-Guest-Mode, X-SupremeAI-Key`.
+
+**Header-by-header preflight isolation** (OPTIONS `/api/v1/auth/register`, Origin pages.dev):
+
+| Requested `Access-Control-Request-Headers` | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `content-type,x-correlation-id,x-supremeai-key` (allowed set) | **200** |
+| `content-type,x-request-id` | **400** |
+| `content-type,x-csrf-token` | **400** |
+| `content-type,x-device-fingerprint` | **400** |
+| `content-type,x-conversation-id` | **400** |
+
+- Chromium fails preflight on non-2xx ⇒ the actual request never fires ⇒ `TypeError: Failed to fetch`.
+- Browser impact (Playwright, 2026-10-28 ~20:00Z, 4 attempts): every `POST /api/v1/auth/register` shows
+  `responseStatus: n/a` in Performance resource timing (~55ms = preflight-only failure); console logs
+  `API_INTERCEPTOR_FETCH_ERROR: Failed to fetch` ×N; `GET /api/config/public` fails identically ⇒
+  `[Config] Deadline 8000ms exceeded. Falling back to safe defaults.`
+- **The UI renders zero feedback** for these failures (no `[role=alert]`, body text unchanged;
+  screenshot `c22-register-silent-network-fail.png`) — same silent-failure pattern as #2207/F-7.
+- Controls proving it is NOT the endpoint/network: same browser session `GET /api/health-aggregation → 200`,
+  `GET /api/config/public (simple, no preflight) → 200`, in-page `POST /api/v1/auth/login → 401` (control),
+  and curl POST register → 200. Only preflighted requests carrying non-allow-listed headers die.
+- Re-attribution note: cycle-19 F-1b logged `OPTIONS → 400 + TypeError` and we partially attributed it to
+  a Render cold-start window. The deterministic header-by-header 400s above show the same signature;
+  cold-start 503s also exist (browser observed `GET /health → 503` this cycle), but F-1b's 400-preflight
+  observations were most likely this allow-list bug. Fixer lane should treat "cold-start preflight 400"
+  reports accordingly.
+
+**Smallest safe fix (server, one middleware change)**: extend the preflight handler to (a) return **2xx**
+and (b) set `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` to the union of the allow-list and the app's real headers
+(`X-Request-ID, X-CSRF-Token, X-Device-Fingerprint, X-Trusted-Browser, X-JIT-OTP, X-Conversation-Id`) — or
+simply reflect back `Access-Control-Request-Headers` (standard `cors()` default). No frontend change.
+
+**Regression test**: integration — for every mutating endpoint, preflight echoing the app's exact header
+set must return 2xx with allow-headers ⊇ requested; E2E smoke — headless-browser register reaches a real
+HTTP status (200/4xx), never `Failed to fetch`.
+
+## F-11 — Register succeeds but the account can never log in; register-issued token is dead on arrival (P1)
+
+Same session, warm API, minutes apart (curl, Origin pages.dev):
+
+1. `POST /api/v1/auth/register` `{name, email: qa-e2e-<ts>@example.com, password, confirmPassword}` →
+   **200** `{"token":"sk-supreme-user-…","user_id":"usr-…"}` (token redacted)
+2. `POST /api/v1/auth/login` same email + same password → **401** `{"detail":"Invalid email or password"}`
+3. `username`-variant payload → **401** (not a payload-shape artifact)
+4. `GET /api/v1/auth/me` with `Authorization: Bearer <register token>` → **401**
+   `{"detail":"Invalid or expired session token"}`
+
+Register creates the user record and mints a token, but **neither the login store nor the session store
+knows about it**. Earlier registration (8 min prior) also 401s on login ⇒ not propagation delay.
+
+**Smallest safe fix**: make register persist credentials to the same auth store login reads (and issue
+session tokens from the same issuer/session store `auth/me` validates); add an integration test that
+registers then immediately logs in and calls `auth/me` end-to-end.
+
+**Regression test**: E2E register → login → `auth/me` → 200 chain on a fresh account; contract test
+asserting register-issued token validates on `auth/me`.
+
+## F-9 — Server ignores password-confirmation mismatch (P2)
+
+`POST /api/v1/auth/register` with `password:"…x"` / `confirmPassword:"…DIFFERENT"` → **200 + account
+created** (control: allowed-header preflight ⇒ reachable server-side; the browser could not even send it
+due to F-12). The confirm-password rule is enforced nowhere: no client guard (bundle fired the API call
+despite mismatch — Performance evidence) and no server check.
+
+**Smallest safe fix**: server-side equality check returning 422 `{"detail":"Passwords do not match"}`;
+optionally add the same client check, but server-side is the safety net.
+
+## F-10 — No duplicate-email guard: same email registers repeatedly into new accounts (P2)
+
+`POST /api/v1/auth/register` twice with identical email → **200 both times**, different `user_id`
+(`usr-…545` then `usr-…582`). No 4xx, no verification email anywhere in the flow. Consequences:
+orphaned duplicate identities per email; ownership of an email is never established; combined with
+F-11 this leaves users with multiple dead accounts and no path in.
+
+**Smallest safe fix**: unique constraint on email in the auth store → 409 `{"detail":"Email already
+registered"}`; surface the existing shared alert component (same pattern as #2207/F-7 fix).
+
+## Journey impact (same session, same bundle)
+
+| Step | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Passkey sign-in | 405 (filed #2207/F-1a) |
+| Google sign-in | button disabled "coming soon" |
+| Register from UI | preflight 400 → Failed to fetch → **silent** (F-12) |
+| Register via API | 200 but token dead + login 401 (F-11) |
+| Login any registered cred | 401 (F-11) |
+| Error feedback on any of the above | none (filed #2207/F-7; re-confirmed on /register this cycle) |
+
+⇒ a brand-new user cannot reach the product through any advertised path. PASS controls for scope honesty:
+login preflight with allowed headers ⇒ 200 (login path is CORS-clean today); `GET /api/health-aggregation`
+⇒ 200 in-browser; FCP `/register` 92/120ms; keyboard/focus PASS (5×Tab → first field, `solid 2px` outline);
+mobile 375×812 layout complete (`c21-register-mobile.png` prior cycle, layout unchanged).
+
+## Why
+
+Four independent server-side defects compound into a total activation outage that no amount of UI work
+can route around — and the UI's silence (filed separately in #2207) hides all of them from users and from
+console-only monitoring. The CORS allow-list drift is the kind of regression that passes every unit test
+and breaks every real browser, which is why the preflight-with-real-headers integration test matters.
+
+## Alternatives rejected
+
+- **Frontend-only workaround (drop custom headers / send only allow-listed ones)**: hides F-12 but breaks
+  request tracing, CSRF and device-fingerprint features the headers exist for; server allow-list fix is
+  one line and matches standard `cors()` behavior.
+- **Public CORS proxy / Pages Function relay**: new infrastructure and a second hop; the API already has
+  correct CORS middleware — it needs its header list updated.
+- **Filing the four findings as four separate WOs**: they are one broken journey with one fix branch
+  (auth API); a single WO with per-finding regression tests avoids four fixers colliding on the same file.
+- **Treating F-1b as cold-start only (Cycle-19 interpretation)**: retained cold-start 503 evidence, but
+  the deterministic 400-by-header isolation supersedes it for preflight failures.
